 "retrain_reading": "In scenario A every decision agrees with the dataset labels. Confirming botnets the model already scores near 100 % adds little information, so F1 barely moves for the baseline (+0.2 points) and GraphSAGE (+0.1) and falls for GCN (-2.1) and GAT (-0.9). With so few labels these changes are of the size of the seed-to-seed variation in Section 8.2.1, so the run shows that the loop works and does not damage the model, not that it improves it. One side effect is a defect worth recording: after this run the application served GAT instead of GraphSAGE, because the best architecture is picked on the validation F1 of a single seed and the validation set has just lost the reviewed accounts, although GraphSAGE has the higher test F1 after the run (0.947 against 0.892). Retraining can therefore switch the served model to a worse one. In scenario B the two errors cost F1 for every model: GCN falls by 8.2 points, GraphSAGE by 4.1, GAT by 1.5 and the baseline by 0.3, the number of botnets shown drops from 8 to 7 because the 62 accounts of the wrongly dismissed botnet were relabelled genuine, and 534 accounts remain flagged instead of 548. The loop learns whatever it is told: it makes moderator decisions valuable, and moderator errors costly. Two runs with one seed each do not support any statistical statement, but the direction is clear and follows from how the labels are used."
}